diag('optics','Read the DOM',[
 ['An LR4 link’s Rx power reads −28 dBm. The far end’s Tx power is normal, around 0 dBm.','Loss in the path: a dirty or damaged connector, a bad patch cord, or a broken fiber',['The local laser is failing','The module is overheating','The receiver is overloaded'],'The far end is sending and the light is not arriving, so the loss is in between. Clean and inspect first: Arista’s transceiver guide names dirty connectors as a major cause of link failures.'],
 ['A 10 km LR optic is used on a 3 m patch between two switches in the same rack. Rx power reads +1.5 dBm and the link takes errors.','Receiver overload: too much light on a short link, so add an attenuator',['The fiber is too long','The connectors are dirty','The wavelength is wrong'],'Long-reach transmitters are strong. Arista’s guide warns that shorter links need attenuation to protect the receiver from permanent damage.'],
 ['Over several months, Tx bias current has crept up while Tx power slowly fell.','The laser is aging: the module drives more current to hold output and is nearing end of life',['A dirty connector at the far end','Receiver overload','A wrong FEC setting'],'Bias current and Tx power are the laser’s vital signs. Rising bias with falling power is classic wear-out; replace it before it fails.'],
 ['Module temperature reads 78 °C and the link flaps.','A cooling problem: blocked airflow, wrong fan direction, or a high-power module in a port that can’t cool it',['A fiber break','The wrong breakout mode','Receiver overload'],'Many modules have a 70–75 °C maximum case temperature, and 800G modules draw up to 16 W. Check airflow direction and port power support.'],
 ['DOM looks perfect at both ends (lasers on, Rx power normal) but the link stays down.','A configuration mismatch: speed, FEC mode or breakout setting differs between the two ends',['A dirty connector','Laser aging','A fiber break'],'Healthy optical levels rule out the light path. Check that both ends agree on speed, FEC and lane mapping.'],
 ['Rx power is normal, but pre-FEC BER has been climbing toward the FEC threshold. Post-FEC is still clean.','The link is degrading but FEC still corrects everything: fix it before it crosses the threshold',['Nothing is wrong because post-FEC is clean','The FEC engine is broken','The counters are wrong'],'Pre-FEC BER is the early warning. Past the threshold, uncorrectable errors appear suddenly. For 400ZR, Arista lists a pre-FEC threshold of 1e−2 (C-FEC).'],
 ['On a parallel DR4 link, three lanes show normal Rx power and one lane reads about −40 dBm.','One fiber in the MPO trunk is dirty, broken or mis-mated, or that lane’s laser is dead',['All four lasers are aging','The switch port is misconfigured','The module is overheating'],'Per-lane DOM pinpoints the bad fiber. −40 dBm is essentially no light at all.'],
 ['A 400G-VSR4 link over an MPO-12 trunk that used to carry 100G-SR4 shows high loss and errors.','Connector polish mismatch: VSR4 uses angled (APC) MPOs, while SR4 used flat (UPC) ones',['VSR4 needs single-mode fiber','The trunk is too short','SR4 optics damaged the fiber'],'Arista’s FAQ notes 400G-VSR4 and 800G-2VSR4 use MPO-12 APC, which is not compatible with the UPC connectors used by SR4 optics.']],
 'Start with DOM at both ends: Tx power, Rx power, bias, temperature. Then clean and inspect before you swap anything.');
bank('optics','Optics and cabling concepts',[
 ['Multimode versus single-mode fiber?','Multimode has a large ~50 µm core for cheap 850 nm short-reach optics; single-mode has a ~9 µm core for long reach at 1310 and 1550 nm',['Multimode carries several wavelengths; single-mode carries one','Single-mode is cheaper and only for short runs','They are the same fiber with different jackets'],'Light takes many paths (modes) down a wide core, and the different path lengths smear pulses, limiting reach to around 100 m at high speed.'],
 ['Why is the 1550 nm region used for long-haul and DWDM?','Fiber loss is lowest there and erbium-doped fiber amplifiers (EDFAs) work in that band',['It is the only wavelength lasers can make','It has zero dispersion','It is the cheapest wavelength to detect'],'The FOA notes amplifiers work around 1520–1560 nm, which is why most DWDM systems are built there.'],
 ['CWDM versus DWDM?','CWDM spaces wavelengths 20 nm apart (few channels, cheap lasers); DWDM spaces them about 0.8 nm (100 GHz) or closer, with many more channels and stable lasers',['CWDM is for single-mode only; DWDM for multimode','DWDM uses one wavelength per fiber','They differ only in connector type'],'From the FOA page: CWDM lasers are 20 nm apart, DWDM 0.8 nm apart, and current systems offer up to 96 or 128 channels.'],
 ['What makes coherent optics “coherent”?','The receiver mixes the signal with its own local laser, recovering amplitude and phase on both polarizations',['The laser is very bright','It uses multimode fiber','It sends each bit twice'],'Recovering phase allows QAM and lets a DSP undo chromatic dispersion electronically, which is how 400ZR reaches 120 km without dispersion compensators.'],
 ['What is OSNR?','Optical signal-to-noise ratio: signal power versus the noise added by optical amplifiers',['The ratio of Tx to Rx power','The bit error rate after FEC','The optical return loss of a connector'],'It is the main limit on amplified coherent links. Arista’s 400ZR datasheet asks for more than 26 dB of OSNR at the receiver.'],
 ['DAC versus AEC versus AOC?','DAC: passive copper, cheapest, a couple of meters; AEC: copper with retimers, a bit longer; AOC: fiber with the optics built into each end, up to tens of meters',['All three are fiber','A DAC needs a separate transceiver at each end','An AOC is copper with active amplifiers'],'For 800G, Arista lists passive DACs at 1–2 m, AECs at 1–3 m (up to 5 m for some), and AOCs from 1 to 30 m.'],
 ['OSFP versus QSFP-DD?','Both carry 8 lanes; OSFP is larger with its own integrated heatsink and runs cooler, QSFP-DD is backward compatible with QSFP and relies on the host’s heatsink',['OSFP carries 4 lanes, QSFP-DD 8','QSFP-DD is only for copper','They plug into each other’s ports'],'From Arista’s 800G FAQ: OSFP has about 50% more surface area and runs roughly 5–15 °C cooler. The two can interoperate over a link but not share a port.'],
 ['What does a gearbox do in an optic?','Converts between lane counts and rates, for example 8 × 50G PAM4 electrical to 4 × 100G PAM4 optical',['Changes the wavelength','Adds error correction only','Steps the supply voltage'],'Arista’s 400G-FR4 and DR4 use an 8:4 gearbox; 400G-SR8 needs none because it has 8 optical lanes.'],
 ['What is LPO (linear pluggable optics)?','An optic with no DSP or retimer: the host’s SerDes drives the optics directly',['An optic for long-haul links','A copper cable with optics in it','A module with two DSPs'],'Removing the DSP saves power and latency, but the host’s SerDes and equalization must handle the whole channel. Arista lists LPO-800G-2DR4 at 500 m.'],
 ['Why do parallel single-mode optics use angled (APC) MPO connectors?','The angled end face sends back-reflections out of the core instead of back into the laser',['APC connectors are cheaper','They carry more fibers','They are required for multimode'],'Reflections disturb lasers and PAM4 receivers. Arista’s FAQ notes MPO-12 APC and MPO-16 APC on its 800G parallel optics.'],
 ['What does DOM (digital optical monitoring) report?','Module temperature, supply voltage, laser bias current, Tx power and Rx power',['Only whether the link is up','Packet counts and errors','The MAC address of the far end'],'These five readings, at both ends, are the first thing to check on any optical link problem.'],
 ['What does the “2” in OSFP-800G-2FR4 mean?','Two independent 400G-FR4 ports in one module',['Two wavelengths','A 2 km reach','Second generation'],'Arista’s initial 800G ports run as 2 × 400GE, so one module can serve two separate 400G links.'],
 ['What is FEC, and what does it cost?','Forward error correction: redundant bits let the receiver fix errors, at the cost of some bandwidth overhead and latency',['A way to compress data','A laser safety feature','A type of fiber connector'],'PAM4 links depend on it. Monitoring pre-FEC BER tells you how close a link is to failing while post-FEC is still clean.'],
 ['What is an EDFA?','An erbium-doped fiber amplifier: it boosts all C-band channels at once, optically, without converting to electrical',['A type of laser','A DWDM multiplexer','An electrical repeater'],'Arista’s pluggable amplifiers for 400ZR contain a booster EDFA and a pre-amp EDFA.'],
 ['What is chromatic dispersion?','Different wavelengths travel at slightly different speeds, so pulses spread out over distance',['Light leaking out of a bent fiber','Loss at a connector','Two polarizations mixing'],'Coherent DSPs compensate it electronically; 400ZR tolerates 2400 ps/nm, according to Arista’s datasheet.'],
 ['What does BiDi mean on an optic such as 100G-SRBD?','Two wavelengths travel in opposite directions on each fiber, so fewer fibers are needed',['It works at two speeds','It has two lasers on the same wavelength','It works on both multimode and single-mode'],'BiDi lets a duplex pair carry traffic that would otherwise need a parallel MPO trunk.']],
 'Know the vocabulary cold: form factor, lanes, modulation, reach class, fiber type, connector.');

